:"Mo-Fr 09:00-18:30; Sa 09:00-13:00","w":"https://www.bibliotecacivicahortis.it/","ph":"+39 040 6758200","wifi":1},{"n":"Teatro Lirico Giuseppe Verdi","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.651,"lo":13.769,"d":222,"ds":"The Teatro Lirico Giuseppe Verdi is an opera house located in Trieste, Italy and named after the composer Giuseppe Verdi. Privately constructed, it was inaugurated as the Teatro Nuovo to replace the smaller 800-seat \"Cesareo Regio Teatro di San Pietro\" on 21 April 1801 with a performance of Johann Simon Mayr's Ginevra di Scozia. Initially, the Nuovo had 1,400 seats.","wpu":"Teatro Lirico Giuseppe Verdi","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Teatro_Verdi.JPG/960px-Teatro_Verdi.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Molo Audace","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.6521,"lo":13.7664,"d":225,"ds":"Molo Audace is a pier located on the shores of Trieste, Italy right in the centre of the city, a few steps from Piazza Unità d'Italia and the Grand Canal. It separates the San Giorgio basin from the San Giusto basin of the Old Port.","wpu":"Molo Audace","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Molo_Audace_16-12-2007_001.jpg/960px-Molo_Audace_16-12-2007_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Piazza Unità d'Italia","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.65,"lo":13.767778,"d":346,"ds":"Piazza Unità d'Italia (English: Unity of Italy Square) is the main square in Trieste, a seaport city in northeast Italy. Located at the foot of the hill with the castle of San Giusto, the square faces the Adriatic Sea. It is often said to be Europe's largest square located next to the sea.","wpu":"Piazza Unità d'Italia","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Triest_Piazza_dell%27Unita_d%27Italia.JPG/960px-Triest_Piazza_dell%27Unita_d%27Italia.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Saint Spyridon Serbian Orthodox Church (Trieste)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.651389,"lo":13.773333,"d":381,"ds":"Saint Spyridon Serbian Orthodox Church (Italian: Chiesa ortodossa serba di San Spiridione; Serbian: Српска православна црква светог Спиридона, romanized: Srpska pravoslavna crkva svetog Spiridona) is an Eastern Orthodox church located in Trieste, Italy. It is under jurisdiction of the Serbian Orthodox Eparchy of Switzerland of the Serbian Orthodox Church.","wpu":"Saint Spyridon Serbian Orthodox Church (Trieste)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Trieste_San_Spiridone_4.JPG/960px-Trieste_San_Spiridone_4.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Savoia Excelsior Palace","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.649444,"lo":13.765556,"d":477,"ds":"The Savoia Excelsior Palace is a 4 star hotel in Trieste, Italy owned by the Starhotels group. The hotel is located close to Piazza Unità d'Italia overlooking the Gulf of Trieste and has 142 rooms (including suites and apartments). The hotel has a bar, \"Le Rive\", and a restaurant, \"The Savoy\", which seats up to 150 people.","wpu":"Savoia Excelsior Palace","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Trieste_Savoia_Excelsior_Palace_3.jpg/960px-Trieste_Savoia_Excelsior_Palace_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sant'Antonio Taumaturgo, Trieste","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.6514,"lo":13.7751,"d":506,"ds":"The Church of Sant'Antonio Taumaturgo (commonly known as the Church of Sant'Antonio Nuovo), is the main religious building in the Borgo Teresiano in the centre of Trieste as well as the city's largest Catholic church. It stands on a square also known as Sant’Antonio Nuovo, at the end of the Grand Canal. The building project dates back to 1808, but work only began in 1825.","wpu":"Sant'Antonio Taumaturgo, Trieste","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Trieste_Sant%27Antonio_nuovo_3.JPG/960px-Trieste_Sant%27Antonio_nuovo_3.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Trieste Cathedral","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.646389,"lo":13.7725,"d":783,"ds":"Trieste Cathedral (Italian: Basilica cattedrale di San Giusto Martire), dedicated to Saint Justus, is a Roman Catholic cathedral in Trieste, in northeastern Italy. It is the seat of the Bishop of Trieste. In 1899, Pope Leo XIII granted it the status of a basilica minor.","wpu":"Trieste Cathedral","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/San_Giusto_Cathedral_and_Roman_Forum%2C_Trieste.jpg/960px-San_Giusto_Cathedral_and_Roman_Forum%2C_Trieste.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Revoltella Museum","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.646944,"lo":13.763056,"d":816,"ds":"The Revoltella Museum (Italian: Museo Revoltella) is a modern art gallery founded in Trieste, Italy, in 1872 by Baron Pasquale Revoltella. The baron left his house to the city (located in Piazza Venezia), including all the artworks, furniture and books it contained.","wpu":"Revoltella Museum","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d9/Painting_Palazzo_Revoltella.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Synagogue of Trieste","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.653056,"lo":13.780278,"d":876,"ds":"The Synagogue of Trieste (Italian: Tempio Israelitico di Trieste), also the Great Synagogue of Trieste, is an Orthodox Jewish congregation and synagogue, that is located at Via S. Francesco D'Assisi 19, Trieste, in Friuli-Venezia Giulia, in Northern Italy. Designed by Ruggero Berlam and Arduino Berlam in the Romanesque Revival style, the synagogue was completed in 1912.","wpu":"Synagogue of Trieste","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/La_Sinagoga_-_Trieste.jpg/960px-La_Sinagoga_-_Trieste.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Politeama Rossetti","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.6525,"lo":13.784722,"d":1223,"ds":"Politeama Rossetti is a theatre in the Italian city of Trieste. With over 60 shows scheduled each season, running from October to June, its stage shows include plays, musicals, ballet, dance and rock concerts. It is the home of Teatro Stabile del Friuli Venezia Giulia, one of Italy's oldest and major public theatres, founded in 1954.","wpu":"Politeama Rossetti","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Rossetti02.jpg/960px-Rossetti02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Vittoria Light","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.675633,"lo":13.757008,"d":2683,"ds":"Vittoria Light (Italian: Faro della Vittoria, Slovene: Svetilnik zmage) also known as the Victory Lighthouse, is an active lighthouse in Trieste, Italy, serving the Gulf of Trieste. It is located on the Hill of Gretta (Poggio di Gretta), off the Strada del Friuli. At a height of 223 feet (68 m) it is one of the tallest lighthouses in the world.","wpu":"Vittoria Light","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Vittoria_Light_2005.jpg/960px-Vittoria_Light_2005.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Santissima Trinità, Cattinara","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.6425,"lo":13.8155,"d":3798,"ds":"The Most Holy Trinity Church (Italian: Chiesa della Santissima Trinità) is a Catholic church in Cattinara, a frazione of Trieste, Italy. It is located at the east east of the city. It is part of the deanery of Opicina.","wpu":"Santissima Trinità, Cattinara","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Trieste-Cattinara_011.jpg/960px-Trieste-Cattinara_011.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Temple of Monte Grisa","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.693111,"lo":13.749217,"d":4717,"ds":"The Temple of Monte Grisa, officially the National Shrine of Mary Mother and Queen (Italian: Santuario Nazionale a Maria Madre e Regina, Slovene: Marijino svetišče na Vejni), is a Roman-Catholic church north of the city of Trieste. Located at an altitude of 300 metres (980 ft) on the edge of the Karst Plateau above Barcola and Miramare Castle, it is a most conspicuous landmark.","wpu":"Temple of Monte Grisa","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/b/b0/Santuario_di_Montegrisa_%28III%29_%2839212890885%29.jpg/960px-Santuario_di_Montegrisa_%28III%29_%2839212890885%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Church of Saints John and Paul, Muggia","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.6042,"lo":13.7679,"d":5426,"ds":"The Church of Saints John and Paul (Italian: Chiesa dei Santi Giovanni e Paolo, Slovene: Cerkev sv. Janeza in Pavla) is the main place of Catholic worship in Muggia, in the province of Trieste, seat of the homonymous parish belonging to the diocese of Trieste.","wpu":"Church of Saints John and Paul, Muggia","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Muggia_katedra_4.jpg/960px-Muggia_katedra_4.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Basilica of Saint Mary of the Assumption, Muggia","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.6,"lo":13.7526,"d":6029,"ds":"The Basilica of Saint Mary of the Assumption (Italian: Basilica di Santa Maria Assunta, Slovene: Bazilika sv. Marije Vnebovzete) is the parish church of Muggia Vecchia, in Muggia, near Trieste, Italy. In the past it was believed that the town of Muggia Vecchia had ceased to be inhabited after its destruction by the Genoese of Pagano Doria in 1353.","wpu":"Basilica of Saint Mary of the Assumption, Muggia","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Muggia_032.jpg/960px-Muggia_032.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Grotta Gigante","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.709217,"lo":13.763831,"d":6263,"ds":"Grotta Gigante (\"Giant Cave\", Slovene: Briška jama or Jama pri Briščikih), also known as Riesengrotte or as Grotta di Brisciachi, is a giant cave on the Italian side of the Trieste Karst (Carso), close to the village of Borgo Grotta Gigante or Briščiki in the municipality of Sgonico.","wpu":"Grotta Gigante","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/85/Grotta_Gigante.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Miramare Castle","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":45.7025,"lo":13.712222,"d":7053,"ds":"Miramare Castle (Italian: Castello di Miramare; Slovene: Grad Miramar; German: Schloss Miramar; Spanish: Castillo de Miramar) is a 19th-century castle direct on the Gulf of Trieste between Barcola and Grignano in Trieste, northeastern Italy.","wpu":"Miramare Castle","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Trieste_-_castello_di_Miramare_-_202209132345.jpeg/960px-Trieste_-_castello_di_Miramare_-_202209132345.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Trieste ( tree-EST; Italian: [triˈɛste] ; Friulian: Triest; Slovene: Trst) is a city and seaport in northeast Italy. It is the capital and largest city of the autonomous region of Friuli-Venezia Giulia, as well as of the regional decentralization entity of Trieste. As of 2026, it has a population of 198,622, making it the 17th-largest city in Italy.","wpu":"Trieste","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Trieste_%2828766391880%29.jpg/960px-Trieste_%2828766391880%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}